1012:
The Public Art Board shall be comprised of two classes of members: voting members and non-voting ex-officio members. Only the voting members will count toward establishing a quorum of members. Only voting members may vote or conduct official business on behalf of the Board. A Chair of the Public Art Board shall be elected from the voting members of the Board. Non-voting ex-officio members do not count toward establishing a quorum of members and may not vote or conduct official business on behalf of the Board. Non-voting ex-officio members are to serve on the Board in an advisory capacity to effectuate communication between the Macon-Bibb County government and the voting members of the Board.
The voting members of the Board shall be comprised of three (3) citizens of Macon-Bibb County, which may include representatives from Macon-Bibb County arts, non-profit, and community and economic development organizations, professional artists, art educators, and lay persons. Voting members of the Board shall be nominated to the Board by a member of the Macon-Bibb County Commission and confirmed by a vote of the Macon-Bibb County Commission.
Non-voting ex-officio members of the Board shall be comprised of the following individuals:
(1) The Executive Director of the Macon Arts Alliance, or his/her designee;
(2) The Director of the Macon-Bibb County Parks and Beautification department, or his/her designee;
(3) The Director of the Macon-Bibb County Engineering department, or his/her designee;
(4) The Director of the Macon-Bibb County Economic and Community Development department, or his/her designee; and
(5) The Chief Communications Officer for Macon-Bibb County, or his/her designee.
(Ord. No. O-18-0097, § 1, 11-20-2018; Ord. No. O-18-0097 A, § 1, 6-4-2019; O-20-0006, § 6, 1-21-2020; Ord. No. O-22-0022, § 1, 4-19-2022)

1030:
(a) The Board shall perform the following functions:
(1) Identify and create opportunities to boost morale for Macon-Bibb County employees;
(2) Provide a channel through which Macon-Bibb County employees can raise or discuss issues affecting employee morale, including anonymously where appropriate;
(3) Advise the Macon-Bibb County Commission and staff on employee morale-based issues;
(4) Carry out morale-boosting functions or programs as approved by the Macon-Bibb County Commission;
(5) Recommend to the Mayor, County Manager, and Macon-Bibb County Commission policy or legislative changes that can be made for the purpose of improving employee morale;
(6) Conduct surveys of employee morale approximately every six months; and
(7) Take all other actions as assigned by the Mayor, County Manager, or Macon-Bibb County Commission, within the purview of this Board, as set forth in this Division.
(b) The Board's functions shall not include the following:
(1) Addressing or resolving the interpersonal disputes between particular employees, or between employees and their superiors except by referring such matters to the Human Resources Department;
(2) Addressing or resolving matters relating to the discipline, hiring, termination, salary, assignment, shift placement, or promotion decisions of particular employees, except by referring such matters to the County Human Resources Department;
(3) Addressing or resolving matters concerning particular threatened or pending litigation, administrative complaints, labor disputes, or other state, federal, civil, criminal, or administrative legal claims or actions, except by referring such matters to the County Attorney's Office;
(4) Addressing or resolving matters relating to the budgeting or staffing levels of individual departments, except by referring such matters to the County Manager;
(5) Addressing or resolving any matters of a particular nature, such as matters concerning only one employee or situation, or a small number of isolated employees or situations, except by referring such matters to the Human Resources Department, County Manager, County Attorney's Office, or to other appropriate departments, depending on the circumstances of the submission;
(6) Notwithstanding the foregoing, the Board may study or address matters identified in this subsection (b) to the extent that they may be generalized as affecting morale across many employees or departments. By way of example, and without limitation, addressing a single interpersonal dispute between two employees is outside of the purview of this Board under subsection (b)(1) of this Section; however, if the Board receives a large number of reports about interpersonal disputes, involving many different employees, so as to suggest a larger pattern of employee tensions that affect morale generally, then in addition to referring the individual disputes to the Human Resources Department for handling on an individual level under subsection (b)(1) of this Section, the Board may study or address ways in which to reduce such tensions or improve interpersonal relations among employees generally in order to improve employee morale County-wide.
(Ord. No. O-18-0101, § 1, 11-20-2018)

